Z||000000||rnoswal||09-06-2000||10:33 PM||||I just got an 85 Fiero last week, 4 cyl. I want to autocross it and have a few questions. Currently the engine has TBI. I would like to put a 350 holley on it. Did any of the 2.5,s come with a carb or is there an adapter plate that will fit on the TBI holes that will fit the 350? Also, does the intake manifold flow very well or should I look somewhere for a better manifold? Headers, anyone? What rods come in this engine, 5.7, 6.0 or something else? Lastly cam and rocker ratio. I would like to turn up to 7000 rpm, can this engine do it?<BR>Thanks<BR>Russ <P>------------------<BR>Russ||||reg||5
Z||000001||baldlobo||09-06-2000||10:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rnoswal:<BR><B>I just got an 85 Fiero last week, 4 cyl. I want to autocross it and have a few questions. Currently the engine has TBI. I would like to put a 350 holley on it. Did any of the 2.5,s come with a carb or is there an adapter plate that will fit on the TBI holes that will fit the 350? Also, does the intake manifold flow very well or should I look somewhere for a better manifold? Headers, anyone? What rods come in this engine, 5.7, 6.0 or something else? Lastly cam and rocker ratio. I would like to turn up to 7000 rpm, can this engine do it?<BR>Thanks<BR>Russ <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>to be honest if your looking for 7000 RPM then you would have to either change to a quad4 engine, an SD4 engine or change every internal in the "Duke" to forged and hope the block stays together.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-06-2000||10:56 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<P>Unfortunatly I can't help you much with your question about that kind of setup, but maybe someone here can.  Don't think you can get much more than 5000 rpm out of a 4 safely. <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rnoswal||09-06-2000||10:56 PM||||I race a Toyota Celica with the 22R block and the 20r head. With rod bolts that engine is very reliable at 7000. Is the 2.5 Iron Duke that much of a dud? Is the crank the weak spot? What kind of rpm's can I get out of this engine?<BR>Thanks||||reg||10
Z||000004||whamel||09-06-2000||11:01 PM||||Congrats on your new Fiero!  Let me direct you to probably one of the best references on the 2.5 (Iron Duke) you will find anywhere.  From this link it will pretty much take you where you need to go.  IMHO, you may want to reverse your thinking though and start at the bottom end first if you want that kind of 'go fast' out of a Duke though.  <BR> <A HREF="http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/IronDuke.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/IronDuke.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by whamel (edited 09-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||09-06-2000||11:30 PM||||Yup. The Iron Duck is no japanese 4cyl (or, no american engine is as good as any japanese engine)... uh oh. they hate it when someone speaks the truth. Gotta duck out of here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||rnoswal||09-06-2000||11:49 PM||||Ok, I agree that I need to workon the lower end first. Are there any parts such as rods and pistons that are interchangable with any of the V-8 chevy engines. What are the connecting rods? Are they chevy 5.7 or something else? Does the super duty crank fit right in to this block and if it does, will this allow the engine to rev higher? <BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-07-2000||12:05 AM||||You need to rebuild the entire engine. Even then the thing likely won't turn 7k. If it does it probly won't last long.<P>On the other hand you need to learn some things about engines. RPM doesn't always mean what you apparantly think it does.<P>Jap engines often wind higher than U.S. ones. Doen't mean they are better. Many jap engines can't make torque worth a crud and that's exactly what you need most. You can't get going fast enough for the HP peak to matter most of the time on an autocross coarse. The coarse is more like a series of tiny drag races.<P>Is the duke a dud? No It's just not built for what you have been told you need. you might not get a whole lot mor HP out of it but you can probably increase torque. It's already around 130 somthing FP at about 3200rpm. Not bad at all for alot of 4 cyl motors. Espceially considering these are built for economy not racing. (If it's on an automatic I think the output from the TC is in the 160-180fp range when the TC is at full multiply.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||rnoswal||09-07-2000||12:44 AM||||Thanks Theogre, now I know how you got your name! I really don't need foreign car engine bashing. Automatics are slow and heavy. I need the free rpms so I won't need to be constantly shifting, even though I like to use the engine to help with braking. I just want to hear from other Fiero owners who have undoubtedly used their car the same way I want to, and to find out about performance parts. I have seen your postings on pages and they are pretty informative, so lets keep them that way, OK?!<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000009||DjKid||09-07-2000||01:43 AM||||Why must everyone bash automatics as being slow? someone explain this to me and give a halfway thought out answer and maybe I'll shutup?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djkid_sig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/" TARGET=_blank>'Blue Knight'</A>||||reg||5
Z||000010||Oreif||09-07-2000||02:27 AM||||If you want to scream up to the high RPM range you should consider the SD4. An entire engine built for street/strip use is about $9500. That gets you to 210HP carb'd with the motor stroked to 2.7L. Kansas Racing Products has all the piece parts too that will bolt up to your exsisting 2.5L. (Except heads and block all other parts will interchange) The original 2.5 isn't a strong block. So be cautious in how much you stuff into it. If you use an SD4 block or a 1977 thru 1981 2.5L block the internal webbing is thicker and you can go to 4-bolt mains. In 1983 the 2.5L block was designed to reduce the weight of the engine for the increase in economy. I have seen a car with the older block that was machined to accept an SD4 head and stroked to 2.7L. Engine put out a reliable 155hp and redlined around 6000. <BR>By the way Welcomee to our forum and keep us informed on your progress.<BR>BTW ~ You say you have an 85. So I assume it's a 5-spd. If you want a higher gear ratio and don't mind losing a gear, The 84 Fiero's had a Muncie 4-spd with a 4.10 ratio.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ur2biteme||09-07-2000||02:45 AM||||trying to steer clear of a flame war......<P>anyway.  everything i have seen on the 2.5L 4cyl, fiero or not, (with the exception of the super duty) is that the block, crank, and head are casted weak to say the least. this keeps the engine from being able to put out rpms too much over 5k without doing some extreme life shortening damage.  these engines cant take any kind of boost ether. there was a thread i read a little while back where nitrous was used, and blew the motor.  if you want to get something high reving, you'll need to do an engine swap (quad 4, or 3400 tdc v6), otherwise, you can build the duke to about 130-140 h.p. from what i've seen, and still have some reliability, but it doesn't do much for the redline.  i have an '87 and these have some outrageous torque for a 4, and would probably be killer for autocross in current form.  it just isn't cost effective in the long run to try to build up the duke.  it was built for gas mileage, not performance.<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
